WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.167 --> 00:00:01.410
(pleasant tonal music)

00:00:01.410 --> 00:00:02.850
- [Narrator] As coronavirus closures

00:00:02.850 --> 00:00:04.700
have put businesses on life support,

00:00:04.700 --> 00:00:06.820
jobless claims have surged.

00:00:06.820 --> 00:00:10.460
In some states, more than 20%
of the workforce filed claims,

00:00:10.460 --> 00:00:12.720
but in other states,
the share is much lower,

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:15.300
and experts say the numbers
may still underestimate

00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:17.340
the scope of people who are out of work

00:00:17.340 --> 00:00:19.090
trying to access the benefit.

00:00:19.090 --> 00:00:22.760
- It's taking states a while
to process benefits as is,

00:00:22.760 --> 00:00:23.593
and to add in

00:00:23.593 --> 00:00:25.870
the new Pandemic Unemployment
Assistance benefit,

00:00:25.870 --> 00:00:28.950
the $600 and the 13 additional weeks.

00:00:28.950 --> 00:00:30.200
- [Narrator] Here are
some of the obstacles

00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:32.330
holding states up as they try to process

00:00:32.330 --> 00:00:34.855
the historic number of
unemployment claims.

00:00:34.855 --> 00:00:36.660
(quietly tense orchestral music)

00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:39.120
Challenge number one, technology.

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:42.630
In some states, old technology
has slowed down the process.

00:00:42.630 --> 00:00:45.160
In mid March, Connecticut
residents began to overload

00:00:45.160 --> 00:00:48.040
the state labor department's
website with claims.

00:00:48.040 --> 00:00:49.400
The portal could only handle

00:00:49.400 --> 00:00:52.070
about 8000 benefit applications at a time.

00:00:52.070 --> 00:00:55.860
Once the problem was resolved,
claims increased sharply.

00:00:55.860 --> 00:00:57.980
A spokesperson from
Connecticut's Labor Department

00:00:57.980 --> 00:00:59.540
said that before the spike in claims,

00:00:59.540 --> 00:01:01.370
the state was already in the final stages

00:01:01.370 --> 00:01:02.593
of moving to the cloud.

00:01:03.590 --> 00:01:05.270
Connecticut is far from the only state

00:01:05.270 --> 00:01:06.750
with technology issues.

00:01:06.750 --> 00:01:08.420
The New York State Department of Labor

00:01:08.420 --> 00:01:12.090
had to add computer servers to
address the flood of claims.

00:01:12.090 --> 00:01:14.350
The department's
unemployment claims hotline

00:01:14.350 --> 00:01:17.840
received 8.2 million calls
in the last week of March

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:21.010
compared with 50,000 in a typical week.

00:01:21.010 --> 00:01:23.390
The website recorded 3.4 million visits

00:01:23.390 --> 00:01:26.470
compared with 350,000 normally.

00:01:26.470 --> 00:01:29.600
- Only 16 states have
upgraded off of their 1970s

00:01:31.030 --> 00:01:33.050
COBOL mainframes entirely.

00:01:33.050 --> 00:01:33.940
Then there are a few states

00:01:33.940 --> 00:01:35.910
who've upgraded their benefits systems,

00:01:35.910 --> 00:01:37.810
four states who've upgraded
their benefits system,

00:01:37.810 --> 00:01:39.920
three states have
upgraded their tax system,

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:42.190
but for the most part,
states are still running

00:01:42.190 --> 00:01:45.290
on pretty old technology.

00:01:45.290 --> 00:01:48.020
- [Narrator] Challenge
number two, experience.

00:01:48.020 --> 00:01:50.280
Some states also lack experience

00:01:50.280 --> 00:01:53.280
in setting up the systems for
the new unemployment funds.

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:55.320
According to Evermore, some elements

00:01:55.320 --> 00:01:57.900
of the Pandemic Unemployment
Assistance program

00:01:57.900 --> 00:01:59.830
are based on the disaster program.

00:01:59.830 --> 00:02:01.170
- Disaster unemployment assistance

00:02:01.170 --> 00:02:03.560
has been around since 1974.

00:02:03.560 --> 00:02:05.820
Some states are used to using the system.

00:02:05.820 --> 00:02:08.150
States that have a lot
of natural disasters

00:02:08.150 --> 00:02:11.660
have implemented disaster
unemployment assistance before,

00:02:11.660 --> 00:02:14.430
states like Florida, Louisiana,

00:02:14.430 --> 00:02:19.350
they get hit with a lot of
hurricanes, the Carolinas, right,

00:02:19.350 --> 00:02:22.500
Texas, but some states haven't had

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:24.110
a lot of natural disasters.

00:02:24.110 --> 00:02:25.150
Arizona, for example,

00:02:25.150 --> 00:02:28.400
hasn't had a natural disaster
declaration since 1983,

00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:31.060
and coincidentally, my
colleague just found out

00:02:31.060 --> 00:02:33.290
that they won't have their
computers up and running

00:02:33.290 --> 00:02:36.146
to process these benefits for 18 weeks.

00:02:36.146 --> 00:02:39.020
- [Narrator] Challenge number
three, financial pressure.

00:02:39.020 --> 00:02:40.930
Some states, like Massachusetts,

00:02:40.930 --> 00:02:43.700
are experiencing additional
financial pressures.

00:02:43.700 --> 00:02:45.720
The new federal unemployment programs

00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:48.220
make up to 39 weeks of
unemployment insurance

00:02:48.220 --> 00:02:51.710
available to previously uncovered
groups like gig workers.

00:02:51.710 --> 00:02:54.890
The federal government pays
an additional $600 per week

00:02:54.890 --> 00:02:57.900
for each successful
claim through July 31st,

00:02:57.900 --> 00:02:59.640
but states are still on the hook

00:02:59.640 --> 00:03:01.970
for standard unemployment payments.

00:03:01.970 --> 00:03:05.600
Those payments range from
$213 a week in Mississippi

00:03:05.600 --> 00:03:08.480
to about $550 a week in Massachusetts.

00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:09.980
- [Michelle] The average
weekly benefit amount

00:03:09.980 --> 00:03:13.140
in the United States is about $370 a week.

00:03:13.140 --> 00:03:17.370
The average weekly wage in the
United States is around $970,

00:03:17.370 --> 00:03:22.370
so that $600 represents that
gap to get workers to 100%.

00:03:22.720 --> 00:03:24.420
- [Narrator] The standard
benefits are paid

00:03:24.420 --> 00:03:26.680
by state unemployment trust funds,

00:03:26.680 --> 00:03:29.570
and some states have drawn
down their trust fund balances

00:03:29.570 --> 00:03:32.130
sharply between February and April.

00:03:32.130 --> 00:03:35.120
For example, Massachusetts
spent more than half

00:03:35.120 --> 00:03:38.180
of its unemployment trust
fund balance in that period.

00:03:38.180 --> 00:03:40.660
A spokesman for the
Massachusetts Labor Department

00:03:40.660 --> 00:03:43.320
said the state is committed
to making sure workers

00:03:43.320 --> 00:03:45.620
continue to receive the
benefits they deserve.

00:03:46.580 --> 00:03:48.860
Several states have
burned through their cash

00:03:48.860 --> 00:03:50.330
and may seek additional loans

00:03:50.330 --> 00:03:52.450
from the federal
government to stay afloat.

00:03:52.450 --> 00:03:55.130
More than 20 states aren't
positioned to pay out enough

00:03:55.130 --> 00:03:57.770
in unemployment benefits
in the event of a recession

00:03:57.770 --> 00:03:59.450
according to the Journal's analysis

00:03:59.450 --> 00:04:03.803
of labor department data.
(pleasant tonal music)

